    "created_at": "2026-07-13T08:43:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-07-13T08:43:36Z",
    "body": "To elaborate, plaintext CR may never appear in **tokenization** inputs because [they're replaced by normalizing newlines (CRLF -> LF, CR -> LF)](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#normalize-newlines).\n\nHowever, [tokenization may _produce_ CR character tokens via numeric character references](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#numeric-character-reference-end-state) that must be handled by **tree construction**: `&#xD;`, `&#13;` or similar.\n\n---\n\nA related point on CR handling is that there's an imbalance between parsing and serialization. The parser removes plaintext CR but preserves CR via character references. However, the [rules for escaping a string](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#serialising-html-fragments) do not include any CR handling (`␍` replaces literal U+000D and `␊` replaces literal U+000A where relevant):\n\n```html\n<p>&#xD;</p>\n<p>&#xD;&#xA;</p>\n<!-- parses as -->\n<p>␍</p>\n<p>␍␊</p>\n<!-- serializes as -->\n<p>␍</p>\n<p>␍␊</p>\n<!-- parses as -->\n<p>␊</p>\n<p>␊</p>\n```\n\nThe roundtrip results in this tree diff:\n\n```diff\n ├─P\n-│ └─#text ␍\n+│ └─#text ␊\n ├─#text ␊\n └─P\n-  └─#text ␍␊\n+  └─#text ␊\n```\n\nThe [serializing HTML fragments](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#serialising-html-fragments) documents that serialization does not necessarily roundtrip the same tree structure. It documents a few cases, but mostly related to the structure of the tree.",
